IMPORT prelude

-- Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 (NSW) -- the provisions a person meets who wants their own health
-- records, wants them corrected, or complains that their health information was mishandled: what the Act protects
-- (ss 5, 6), who may act for them (ss 7, 8), which organisations are bound and where to complain (ss 4, 11, 14, 17, 21,
-- 42(5)), access (HPP 7, s 19(3), Part 4 Division 3), amendment (HPP 8, s 20, Part 4 Division 4), retention (s 25),
-- the limits on use and disclosure (HPP 10, 11, s 16), complaints to the Privacy Commissioner and the Tribunal (Part 6),
-- protection from intimidation (s 70) and fees (s 73).
--
-- SEAT: the individual whose health information it is (a patient, client or former patient; the complainant under
-- Part 6). Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Must they give me my records?", "By when must they
-- answer?", "Can I make them correct my file?", "How long must my records be kept?", "Were they allowed to disclose my
-- information?", "By when must I complain?", "Can I go to NCAT, and by when?", "How much can NCAT award me?". The
-- organisation, the Privacy Commissioner and the Tribunal get no seat: what they must or may do is encoded as what it
-- means for the individual.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "only if", "unless" or "except", a flat "does not"/"must not"/"cannot", a period fixed in
-- the Act's own words), or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a
-- "NOTHING-kind" comment. A judgment the Act leaves to the organisation, the Privacy Commissioner or the Tribunal
-- ("reasonably believes", "is satisfied", "unreasonable", "reasonably necessary", "may allow") is asked as an input and
-- the answer is conditional on it; it is never decided here.
--
-- TIME. "within 45 days after receiving the request", "within 21 days after", "within 6 months ... after", "within 28
-- days after", "for 7 years from" and "until ... attained the age of 25 years" are counted excluding the first day (the
-- Interpretation Act 1987 s 36(1), not deposited): the last day is the day that many days, or the same day of the month
-- that many months, later (fork F1). A last day falling on a weekend or holiday is not moved (fork F2). The commencement
-- of Schedule 1 and Part 6 is not in the deposited text: whether conduct or information falls before or after it is
-- asked. Dates use only the prelude's built-in DATE functions (DATE_FROM_DMY, DATE_SERIAL, DATE_FROM_SERIAL).
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; corpus document dated 27 May 2024; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING.
-- Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Records and Information Privacy Act 2002 -- my health information`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 6: the kinds of personal information that are "health information".
DECLARE `Kind of information` IS ONE OF
    `information or an opinion about my physical or mental health or a disability, at any time (s 6(a)(i))`
    `my express wishes about health services to be provided to me in the future (s 6(a)(ii))`
    `information or an opinion about a health service provided, or to be provided, to me (s 6(a)(iii))`
    `other personal information collected to provide, or in providing, a health service (s 6(b))`
    `other personal information collected in connection with donating my body parts, organs or body substances (s 6(c))`
    `genetic information arising from a health service, in a form that is or could be predictive of my health or a genetic relative's (s 6(d))`
    `a healthcare identifier (s 6(e))`
    `other personal information about me`

-- s 4 "organisation", "public sector agency", "private sector person"; ss 14, 17.
DECLARE `Kind of organisation` IS ONE OF
    `a NSW public sector agency (a government department, statutory body, council, State owned corporation not under the Commonwealth Privacy Act, NSW Health and the like)`
    `a private sector person: a business, company, individual, partnership, trust or association that is not a small business operator or Commonwealth agency`
    `a small business operator within the Commonwealth Privacy Act`
    `a Commonwealth agency within the Commonwealth Privacy Act`
    `ICAC, the NSW Police Force, LECC, the NSW Crime Commission, or the ICAC or LECC Inspector or their staff`
    `an individual dealing with the information only for personal, family or household affairs`

DECLARE `Where my complaint goes` IS ONE OF
    `to the agency itself for internal review under Part 5 of the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (s 21)`
    `by written complaint to the Privacy Commissioner under Part 6 (s 42)`
    `the Act does not apply to this organisation or this conduct`

-- s 8(1): authorised representatives.
DECLARE `Who wants to act for me` IS ONE OF
    `my attorney under an enduring power of attorney`
    `my guardian under the Guardianship Act 1987, or my person responsible under Part 5 of that Act`
    `a person with parental responsibility for me`
    `a person otherwise empowered by law to act as my agent or in my best interests`
    `someone else, such as a friend or relative with no legal authority`

-- s 29: the grounds on which a private sector person need not give access.
DECLARE `Ground for refusing access` IS ONE OF
    `no ground: none is relied on`
    `access would pose a serious threat to my own life or health (s 29(a))`
    `access would pose a serious threat to the life or health of another person (s 29(a))`
    `access would have an unreasonable impact on the privacy of other individuals (s 29(b))`
    `the information relates to legal proceedings between us and is not discoverable or is privileged (s 29(c))`
    `access would reveal the organisation's intentions in negotiations with me, other than about a health service, to its unreasonable disadvantage (s 29(d))`
    `access would be unlawful (s 29(e))`
    `denying access is required or authorised by or under law (s 29(f))`
    `access would be likely to prejudice an investigation of possible unlawful activity (s 29(g))`
    `access would be likely to prejudice a law enforcement function (s 29(h))`
    `a law enforcement agency performing a lawful security function asked that access not be given (s 29(i))`
    `the same kind of request was made unsuccessfully before and there are no reasonable grounds for making it again (s 29(j))`
    `I already had access and this is an unreasonable, repeated request in the same manner (s 29(k))`

DECLARE `My access` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation must give me access, without excessive delay or expense`
    `the organisation need not give me access, on the ground relied on`
    `the organisation need not give me access, but I may ask it to give access to a registered medical practitioner I nominate (s 30)`
    `the organisation need not give me access, because it is lawfully authorised or required not to, or the law otherwise permits it`
    `HPP 7 and Part 4 do not reach this information: it was collected before Schedule 1 commenced and is not a kind listed in section 19(3)`

-- s 28.
DECLARE `Form I asked for` IS ONE OF
    `a copy of the information`
    `an opportunity to inspect it and take notes`
    `no particular form`

DECLARE `My form of access` IS ONE OF
    `an accurate summary of the information is enough, because it was collected before section 28 commenced`
    `in the form I asked for`
    `in another form, because the form I asked for may be refused`
    `either a copy or a reasonable opportunity to inspect and take notes`

-- ss 27, 34: where a request for access or amendment stands.
DECLARE `My request's answer` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation answered within 45 days`
    `the organisation is taken to have refused my request, because it did not answer within 45 days`
    `the 45 days have not yet ended`

-- s 30.
DECLARE `My doctor nomination` IS ONE OF
    `the 21 days for nominating a doctor have not ended`
    `I did not nominate a doctor within 21 days after receiving the notice of refusal`
    `the organisation gave my doctor access within 21 days after my nomination`
    `the organisation did not give my doctor access within 21 days after my nomination`
    `the organisation's 21 days to give my doctor access have not ended`

-- s 34(3), HPP 8(1), s 20.
DECLARE `My amendment` IS ONE OF
    `the organisation must amend the information`
    `the organisation may refuse to amend, because it is satisfied the information is not incomplete, incorrect, irrelevant, out of date or misleading (s 34(3)(a))`
    `the organisation may refuse to amend, because it is satisfied my request contains or comes with materially incorrect or misleading matter (s 34(3)(b))`
    `the organisation need not amend, because it is lawfully authorised or required not to, or the law otherwise permits it`
    `HPP 8 does not require an amendment: the information is accurate, relevant, up to date, complete and not misleading`

-- HPP 10, 11, s 16.
DECLARE `Use or disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `use of my information by the organisation (HPP 10)`
    `disclosure of my information to someone else (HPP 11)`

DECLARE `Ground for use or disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `no ground for the use or disclosure is relied on`
    `I consented (para (a))`
    `directly related purpose I would reasonably expect (para (b))`
    `assisting in a stage of an emergency (para (b1))`
    `serious threat to health or welfare (para (c))`
    `genetic information and a threat to a genetic relative (para (c1))`
    `management of health services (para (d))`
    `training (para (e))`
    `research or statistics in the public interest (para (f))`
    `compassionate reasons, to an immediate family member (HPP 11(1)(g))`
    `finding a missing person (HPP 10(1)(g), 11(1)(h))`
    `suspected unlawful activity, unsatisfactory professional conduct or breach of discipline (HPP 10(1)(h), 11(1)(i))`
    `law enforcement (HPP 10(1)(i), 11(1)(j))`
    `investigative agencies (HPP 10(1)(j), 11(1)(k))`
    `prescribed circumstances (HPP 10(1)(k), 11(1)(l))`

-- ss 43(2)(f), 48(3).
DECLARE `My Commonwealth complaint` IS ONE OF
    `I have made no complaint about this to the Commonwealth Privacy Commissioner or a privacy code adjudicator`
    `I made one and it has not been withdrawn`
    `I made one and a determination has been made on it`
    `I made one and withdrew it before any determination`

DECLARE `My complaint's timing` IS ONE OF
    `my complaint is in time`
    `my complaint is late, but the Privacy Commissioner may allow a later time`

DECLARE `My NCAT application's timing` IS ONE OF
    `my application is in time`
    `my application is late under section 48(2)`
    `too early: the report recommends a day after which I may apply, and that day has not passed`

DECLARE `My costs exposure` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal may order me to pay the costs of the inquiry (s 52(3))`
    `section 52(3) does not apply; costs are governed by the Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (s 53)`

-- s 73.
DECLARE `What the fee is for` IS ONE OF
    `giving me a copy of my health information`
    `giving me an opportunity to inspect and take notes`
    `amending my health information at my request`
    `another matter`

-- s 70.
DECLARE `What I was pressed to do` IS ONE OF
    `not to make or pursue, or to withdraw, a request for access to health information`
    `not to make or pursue, or to withdraw, a complaint to the Privacy Commissioner or the Tribunal under Part 6`
    `not to make or pursue, or to withdraw, an application under Part 5 of the PPIP Act about a Health Privacy Principle or code`
    `to give a consent under the Act, or to do without consent an act that needs consent`
    `something else`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules (ss 27(1), 30, 34(1), 42(3), 48(2)).
DECLARE `Health privacy party` IS ONE OF `the individual`, `the organisation`
DECLARE `Health privacy act` IS ONE OF
    `respond to the request for access`
    `respond to the request for amendment`
    `nominate a registered medical practitioner`
    `give the nominated practitioner access`
    `make the complaint to the Privacy Commissioner`
    `apply to the Tribunal`

§§ `Dates`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the calendar date` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus days` `days` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day` PLUS `days`)

GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days in the month of` `the day` MEANS
    DATE_SERIAL `the first of the next month` MINUS DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 (DATE_MONTH `the day`) (DATE_YEAR `the day`))
    WHERE
        `the first of the next month` MEANS
            IF DATE_MONTH `the day` EQUALS 12
            THEN DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 (DATE_YEAR `the day` PLUS 1)
            ELSE DATE_FROM_DMY 1 (DATE_MONTH `the day` PLUS 1) (DATE_YEAR `the day`)

-- READING (fork F1): a period of months or years ends on the same day of the month that many months later, or on
-- that month's last day where it is shorter (29 February 2028 plus 7 years is 28 February 2035).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` `plus months` `months` MEANS
    DATE_FROM_DMY `the smaller day` `the month` `the year`
    WHERE
        `months from year zero` MEANS (DATE_YEAR `the day`) TIMES 12 PLUS (DATE_MONTH `the day` MINUS 1) PLUS `months`
        `the year` MEANS FLOOR (`months from year zero` DIVIDED BY 12)
        `the month` MEANS `months from year zero` MINUS (`the year` TIMES 12) PLUS 1
        `the smaller day` MEANS
            IF DATE_DAY `the day` AT MOST `the number of days in the month of` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 `the month` `the year`)
            THEN DATE_DAY `the day`
            ELSE `the number of days in the month of` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 `the month` `the year`)

GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE, `the second day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first day` `is after` `the second day` IF DATE_SERIAL `the first day` GREATER THAN DATE_SERIAL `the second day`

§§ `Part 1 -- what the Act protects, and who may act for me`

-- s 5(1) "personal information means information or an opinion ... about an individual whose identity is apparent or
-- can reasonably be ascertained from the information or opinion." (3) "Personal information does not include ... (a)
-- information about an individual who has been dead for more than 30 years, (b)-(o) [publications, library and archive
-- holdings, witness protection, interception warrants, public interest disclosures, controlled operations, Royal
-- Commissions, police complaints, Cabinet information, suitability for public appointment, employee records held by a
-- private sector person, prescribed classes]". s 6 "health information means (a)-(e) ... but does not include health
-- information ... prescribed as exempt health information". A "means" definition and a flat "does not include": JUST
-- FALSE outside them. Whether information falls in an excluded class of s 5(3)(b)-(o) is asked as one fact.
@export Is this information about me "health information" that the Health Records and Information Privacy Act protects (ss 5, 6)?
GIVEN `my identity is apparent or can reasonably be ascertained from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information or opinion is about you and your identity is apparent or can reasonably be worked out from it (it need not be written down)
      `the kind of information` IS A `Kind of information` @desc What the information is about
      `the person it is about has been dead for more than 30 years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the information is about died more than 30 years ago (answer no for yourself)
      `it falls in a class excluded by section 5(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is in a generally available publication, a library, gallery, museum or archive collection, a public State record, a witness protection matter, a telecommunications warrant, a public interest disclosure, a controlled operation, a Royal or Special Commission, a police complaint, Cabinet information, information about suitability for public sector appointment, an employee record held by a private employer, or a prescribed class
      `it is prescribed as exempt health information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether regulations prescribe this information or class of document as exempt health information
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this my health information under the Act` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my identity is apparent or can reasonably be ascertained from it`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `the person it is about has been dead for more than 30 years` OR `it falls in a class excluded by section 5(3)`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `it is prescribed as exempt health information`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of information`
                   WHEN `other personal information about me` THEN JUST FALSE
                   OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 7(1) incapable "if the individual is incapable (despite the provision of reasonable assistance by another person) by
-- reason of age, injury, illness, physical or mental impairment of (a) understanding the general nature and effect of
-- the act, or (b) communicating the individual's intentions". (2) "An authorised representative ... may do such an act
-- on behalf of an individual who is incapable". (3) "may not do such an act on behalf of an individual who is capable
-- ..., unless the individual expressly authorises". s 8(1) "authorised representative ... means (a)-(d)"; (c) "if the
-- individual is a child" (under 18, s 8(3)); (2) "not an authorised representative ... to the extent that acting ... is
-- inconsistent with an order made by a court or tribunal". Capacity is a judgment about facts: asked.
@export Can this person make requests, give consents or complain for me under the health privacy Act (ss 7, 8)?
GIVEN `who wants to act for me` IS A `Who wants to act for me` @desc The person's legal relationship to you
      `I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18 years of age
      `acting for me would be inconsistent with a court or tribunal order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court or tribunal order makes that person's acting for you inconsistent with it
      `I am incapable of doing the act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, even with reasonable help, you cannot understand the general nature and effect of the act, or cannot communicate your intentions about it, because of age, injury, illness or impairment
      `I have expressly authorised them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have expressly authorised that person to do this act for you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this person act for me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is an authorised representative`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `acting for me would be inconsistent with a court or tribunal order`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `I am incapable of doing the act`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE IF `I have expressly authorised them`
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `is an authorised representative` MEANS
            CONSIDER `who wants to act for me`
            WHEN `a person with parental responsibility for me` THEN `I am under 18`
            WHEN `someone else, such as a friend or relative with no legal authority` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `Parts 2, 3 and 6 -- who is bound, and where I complain`

-- s 11(1) "This Act applies to every organisation that is a health service provider or that collects, holds or uses
-- health information." s 4 "private sector person ... does not include a small business operator ... or an agency
-- within the meaning of [the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth)]". s 14 "Nothing in this Act applies" to an individual's personal,
-- family or household affairs. s 17 "This Act does not apply to [ICAC, ... NSW Police Force, LECC, ... Crime
-- Commission] except in connection with the exercise of their administrative and educative functions." s 21(1)
-- contraventions by a public sector agency are "conduct to which Part 5 (Review of certain conduct) of the PPIP Act
-- applies"; (3) "applies only to conduct engaged in after the commencement of this section". s 42(1) a complaint "may
-- be made to the Privacy Commissioner about the alleged contravention ... by a private sector person"; (2) "in
-- writing"; (5) "This Part does not apply to any conduct that occurred before the commencement of this Part." The
-- commencement dates are not in the text: asked. READING (fork F3): the NSW Police Force is a public sector agency
-- (s 4(e)) but s 17 lifts the Act save for administrative and educative functions; there the s 21 route is taken.
@export Does the health privacy Act apply to the organisation that handled my health information, and where do I take my complaint (ss 4, 11, 14, 17, 21, 42)?
GIVEN `the organisation` IS A `Kind of organisation` @desc What kind of organisation (or person) handled your health information
      `it provides health services or collects, holds or uses health information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation provides a health service, or collects, holds or uses health information
      `the conduct was in its administrative or educative functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For ICAC, the NSW Police Force, LECC, the Crime Commission or the Inspectors: whether the conduct was part of their administrative or educative functions (otherwise any answer)
      `the conduct happened after the complaint provisions commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct happened after section 21 (public sector) or Part 6 (private sector) commenced (both in 2004 for most organisations)
GIVETH A `Where my complaint goes`
`Where do I take my health privacy complaint` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it provides health services or collects, holds or uses health information`)
    THEN `the Act does not apply to this organisation or this conduct`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the conduct happened after the complaint provisions commenced`)
         THEN `the Act does not apply to this organisation or this conduct`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the organisation`
              WHEN `a NSW public sector agency (a government department, statutory body, council, State owned corporation not under the Commonwealth Privacy Act, NSW Health and the like)` THEN `to the agency itself for internal review under Part 5 of the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (s 21)`
              WHEN `a private sector person: a business, company, individual, partnership, trust or association that is not a small business operator or Commonwealth agency` THEN `by written complaint to the Privacy Commissioner under Part 6 (s 42)`
              WHEN `ICAC, the NSW Police Force, LECC, the NSW Crime Commission, or the ICAC or LECC Inspector or their staff` THEN
                  IF `the conduct was in its administrative or educative functions`
                  THEN `to the agency itself for internal review under Part 5 of the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 (s 21)`
                  ELSE `the Act does not apply to this organisation or this conduct`
              OTHERWISE `the Act does not apply to this organisation or this conduct`

§§ `Access to my health information (HPP 7, s 19(3), Part 4 Division 3)`

-- HPP 7(1) "An organisation that holds health information must, at the request of the individual to whom the
-- information relates and without excessive delay or expense, provide the individual with access". (2) not required if
-- "lawfully authorised or required not to comply" or "non-compliance is otherwise permitted ... under an Act or any
-- other law"; s 23 the same for Part 4. s 19(3) HPP 7 and Part 4 Division 3 apply to all information collected after
-- Schedule 1 commenced, and to information collected before it only if it is one of the kinds in (a)-(h) (history,
-- findings, investigation results, diagnosis, management plan, action taken or services provided, donation
-- information, predictive genetic information). s 29 "A private sector person is not required to provide ... access
-- ... if (a)-(k)". s 30(1)-(2): where refused because "access would pose a serious threat to the life or health of the
-- individual", the individual "may request ... access ... to a registered medical practitioner nominated by the
-- individual". s 22(3): for a public sector agency the conditions and limitations of the GIPA Act and the PPIP Act on
-- access apply (neither deposited): a ground relied on by a public sector agency gives NOTHING. Whether a ground is made
-- out is a judgment (often "in accordance with guidelines"): asked.
@export Must the organisation that holds my health information give me access to it (HPP 7, ss 19(3), 22, 23, 29, 30)?
GIVEN `the organisation is a public sector agency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a NSW public sector agency (otherwise a private sector person, such as a private hospital, GP clinic or insurer)
      `it was collected before Schedule 1 commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was collected before the Health Privacy Principles commenced (1 September 2004 for most organisations)
      `it is a kind listed in section 19(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a health or illness history, examination findings, investigation results, a diagnosis, a management plan, action taken or services provided by or for a health service provider, organ or tissue donation information, or predictive genetic information
      `the ground relied on` IS A `Ground for refusing access` @desc The ground, if any, the organisation relies on to refuse access
      `the ground is made out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that ground actually holds on the facts (and, where guidelines apply, the refusal follows them); a tribunal decides, answer as you expect
      `it is lawfully authorised or required not to give access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is lawfully authorised or required not to give access, or an Act or other law otherwise permits it not to
GIVETH A MAYBE `My access`
`Must they give me access to my health information` MEANS
    IF `it was collected before Schedule 1 commenced` AND (NOT `it is a kind listed in section 19(3)`)
    THEN JUST `HPP 7 and Part 4 do not reach this information: it was collected before Schedule 1 commenced and is not a kind listed in section 19(3)`
    ELSE IF `it is lawfully authorised or required not to give access`
         THEN JUST `the organisation need not give me access, because it is lawfully authorised or required not to, or the law otherwise permits it`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the ground relied on`
              WHEN `no ground: none is relied on` THEN JUST `the organisation must give me access, without excessive delay or expense`
              OTHERWISE
                  IF `the organisation is a public sector agency`
                  THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: s 22(3), the GIPA Act and the PPIP Act's conditions on access, not deposited)
                  ELSE IF (NOT `the ground is made out`)
                       THEN JUST `the organisation must give me access, without excessive delay or expense`
                       ELSE CONSIDER `the ground relied on`
                            WHEN `access would pose a serious threat to my own life or health (s 29(a))` THEN JUST `the organisation need not give me access, but I may ask it to give access to a registered medical practitioner I nominate (s 30)`
                            OTHERWISE JUST `the organisation need not give me access, on the ground relied on`

-- s 27(1) "A private sector person must respond to a request for access within 45 days after receiving the request."
-- (2) responds by "(a) providing access ... or (b) refusing access". (6) "taken to have refused access ... if [it]
-- fails to respond to the request for access as required by this section." READING (fork F4): a written notice under
-- s 27(4) that access will be given on payment of a fee, given within the 45 days, counts as a response.
@export What is the last day for a private health organisation to answer my request for access to my health information (s 27(1))?
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request for access
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to answer my access request` MEANS `the day it received my request` `plus days` 45

GIVEN `the last day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Health privacy party` `Health privacy act`
`s 27(1) -- a private sector person must respond to a request for access within 45 days` `the last day` MEANS
    PARTY `the organisation`
    MUST `respond to the request for access`
    WITHIN `the last day`
    LEST BREACH BY `the organisation` BECAUSE "request for access not responded to within 45 days after receiving it; taken to have refused access (s 27(1), (6))"

@export Has the private health organisation answered my access request in time, or is it taken to have refused (s 27(1), (6))?
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request for access
      `it has responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has given you access, refused in writing, or given you written notice that access will be given on payment of a fee
      `the day it responded` IS A DATE @desc The day it responded (otherwise any date)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `My request's answer`
`Has my access request been answered in time` MEANS
    IF `it has responded`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 27(1) -- a private sector person must respond to a request for access within 45 days` `the last day`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day answered`), (EVENT `the organisation` `respond to the request for access` `the day answered`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the organisation answered within 45 days`
         OTHERWISE `the organisation is taken to have refused my request, because it did not answer within 45 days`
    ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `today` GREATER THAN `the last day`
         THEN `the organisation is taken to have refused my request, because it did not answer within 45 days`
         ELSE `the 45 days have not yet ended`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to answer my access request` `the day it received my request`)
        `the day answered` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day it responded`

-- s 27(4) "A private sector person who charges a fee for providing access ... need not provide access until 7 days after
-- payment of the fee, if (a) [it] has given the individual written notice stating that access will be provided on
-- payment of a specified fee, and (b) that notice is given within 45 days after receiving a request." READING (fork
-- F5): the answer is the first day on which the organisation can be required to have given access; the Act sets no
-- later day. Where the notice was late the postponement does not apply: NOTHING (there is no s 27(4) day).
@export I have paid the fee for access: from what day can the private health organisation no longer hold back access (s 27(4))?
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request for access
      `the day it gave me the fee notice` IS A DATE @desc The day it gave you written notice that access will be provided on payment of a specified fee
      `the day I paid the fee` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`The day access can no longer be held back after I pay` MEANS
    IF `the day it gave me the fee notice` `is after` (`The last day to answer my access request` `the day it received my request`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the s 27(4) condition fails: the fee notice was not given within 45 days, so s 27(4) gives no day; s 27(6) governs)
    ELSE JUST (`the day I paid the fee` `plus days` 7)

-- s 28(1) access "by giving the individual a copy ... or ... a reasonable opportunity to inspect and take notes". (2)
-- "If an individual has requested that access ... be provided in a particular form, the private sector person is to
-- provide access in that form". (3) may refuse that form if it "(a) would place unreasonable demands on the
-- organisation's resources, or (b) would be detrimental to the preservation of the information or ... would otherwise
-- not be appropriate, or (c) would involve an infringement of copyright"; then "the information is to be provided in
-- another form". (4) "Despite anything to the contrary in this Part or HPP 7, a private sector person who receives a
-- request for access to health information collected before the commencement of this section need only give the
-- individual an accurate summary".
@export In what form must a private health organisation give me access to my health information (s 28)?
GIVEN `the form I asked for` IS A `Form I asked for` @desc The form of access you asked for in your request
      `it was collected before section 28 commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was collected before section 28 commenced (1 September 2004 for most organisations)
      `the form I asked for would be unreasonable or inappropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving access in the form you asked for would place unreasonable demands on the organisation's resources, harm the preservation of the information or otherwise be inappropriate, or infringe copyright (answer as you expect a tribunal to)
GIVETH A `My form of access`
`In what form will I get access` MEANS
    IF `it was collected before section 28 commenced`
    THEN `an accurate summary of the information is enough, because it was collected before section 28 commenced`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the form I asked for`
         WHEN `no particular form` THEN `either a copy or a reasonable opportunity to inspect and take notes`
         OTHERWISE
             IF `the form I asked for would be unreasonable or inappropriate`
             THEN `in another form, because the form I asked for may be refused`
             ELSE `in the form I asked for`

-- s 30(2) the individual "may request the private sector person to give access to the information to a registered
-- medical practitioner nominated by the individual." (3) "The request is to be made within 21 days after the notice of
-- refusal was received." (5) "The private sector person must provide access to the health information to the nominated
-- registered medical practitioner within 21 days after being advised by the individual of the nomination". The
-- second duty runs from the nomination, so it sits under HENCE.
@export What is the last day for nominating a doctor to see the health information I was refused (s 30(3))?
GIVEN `the day I received the notice of refusal` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the organisation's notice refusing access because of a serious threat to your life or health
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to nominate a doctor` MEANS `the day I received the notice of refusal` `plus days` 21

GIVEN `the last day to nominate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Health privacy party` `Health privacy act`
`s 30(3), (5) -- nominate a practitioner within 21 days; access to be given to them within 21 days after` `the last day to nominate` MEANS
    PARTY `the individual`
    MUST `nominate a registered medical practitioner`
    WITHIN `the last day to nominate`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the organisation`
        MUST `give the nominated practitioner access`
        WITHIN 21
        LEST BREACH BY `the organisation` BECAUSE "access not given to the nominated registered medical practitioner within 21 days after being advised of the nomination (s 30(5))"
    LEST BREACH BY `the individual` BECAUSE "no medical practitioner nominated within 21 days after the notice of refusal was received (s 30(3))"

@export I was refused access because it would seriously threaten my life or health: where does my nomination of a doctor stand (s 30)?
GIVEN `the day I received the notice of refusal` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the notice refusing access
      `I have nominated a doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have told the organisation which registered medical practitioner should be given access
      `the day I nominated` IS A DATE @desc The day you advised the organisation of the nomination (otherwise any date)
      `my doctor has been given access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has given your nominated doctor access
      `the day my doctor was given access` IS A DATE @desc The day it gave your doctor access (otherwise any date)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `My doctor nomination`
`Where does my doctor nomination stand` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I have nominated a doctor`)
    THEN IF DATE_SERIAL `today` GREATER THAN `the last day to nominate`
         THEN `I did not nominate a doctor within 21 days after receiving the notice of refusal`
         ELSE `the 21 days for nominating a doctor have not ended`
    ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `the day I nominated` GREATER THAN `the last day to nominate`
         THEN `I did not nominate a doctor within 21 days after receiving the notice of refusal`
         ELSE IF `my doctor has been given access`
              THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 30(3), (5) -- nominate a practitioner within 21 days; access to be given to them within 21 days after` `the last day to nominate`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the nomination`), (EVENT `the individual` `nominate a registered medical practitioner` `the nomination`), (WAITUNTIL `the access`), (EVENT `the organisation` `give the nominated practitioner access` `the access`))
                   WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the organisation gave my doctor access within 21 days after my nomination`
                   OTHERWISE `the organisation did not give my doctor access within 21 days after my nomination`
              ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `today` GREATER THAN `the nomination` PLUS 21
                   THEN `the organisation did not give my doctor access within 21 days after my nomination`
                   ELSE `the organisation's 21 days to give my doctor access have not ended`
    WHERE
        `the last day to nominate` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to nominate a doctor` `the day I received the notice of refusal`)
        `the nomination` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day I nominated`
        `the access` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day my doctor was given access`

§§ `Amendment of my health information (HPP 8, s 20, Part 4 Division 4)`

-- HPP 8(1) an organisation "must, at the request of the individual ..., make appropriate amendments ... to ensure that
-- the health information (a) is accurate, and (b) ... is relevant, up to date, complete and not misleading." (4) not
-- required if "lawfully authorised or required not to comply" or non-compliance is "otherwise permitted ... under an
-- Act or any other law". s 20: HPP 8 "applies to public sector agencies despite HPP 8 (4) and section 21 of the State
-- Records Act 1998": a public sector agency cannot rely on HPP 8(4). s 34(3) a private sector person "may refuse to
-- amend ... (a) if it is satisfied that the health information is not incomplete, incorrect, irrelevant, out of date
-- or misleading, or (b) if it is satisfied that the request contains or is accompanied by matter that is incorrect or
-- misleading in a material respect"; s 23 lifts Part 4 where lawfully authorised. Whether the information is in fact
-- deficient, and what the organisation is satisfied of, are judgments: asked. READING (fork F6): for a public sector
-- agency, HPP 8 turns on whether the information is in fact deficient; for a private sector person on s 34(3)'s
-- satisfaction, with HPP 8(1) the duty where no refusal ground holds.
@export Must the organisation correct my health information as I have asked (HPP 8, ss 20, 23, 34(3))?
GIVEN `the organisation is a public sector agency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a NSW public sector agency (otherwise a private sector person)
      `the information is inaccurate, irrelevant, out of date, incomplete or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is in fact inaccurate, or (for the purpose it was collected or is used for) irrelevant, out of date, incomplete or misleading
      `it is satisfied the information is not deficient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a private organisation: whether it is satisfied the information is not incomplete, incorrect, irrelevant, out of date or misleading
      `it is satisfied my request contains materially incorrect or misleading matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a private organisation: whether it is satisfied your request contains, or comes with, matter that is incorrect or misleading in a material respect
      `it is lawfully authorised or required not to amend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is lawfully authorised or required not to amend, or an Act or other law otherwise permits it not to
GIVETH A `My amendment`
`Must they correct my health information` MEANS
    IF `the organisation is a public sector agency`
    THEN IF `the information is inaccurate, irrelevant, out of date, incomplete or misleading`
         THEN `the organisation must amend the information`
         ELSE `HPP 8 does not require an amendment: the information is accurate, relevant, up to date, complete and not misleading`
    ELSE IF `it is lawfully authorised or required not to amend`
         THEN `the organisation need not amend, because it is lawfully authorised or required not to, or the law otherwise permits it`
         ELSE IF `it is satisfied the information is not deficient`
              THEN `the organisation may refuse to amend, because it is satisfied the information is not incomplete, incorrect, irrelevant, out of date or misleading (s 34(3)(a))`
              ELSE IF `it is satisfied my request contains materially incorrect or misleading matter`
                   THEN `the organisation may refuse to amend, because it is satisfied my request contains or comes with materially incorrect or misleading matter (s 34(3)(b))`
                   ELSE IF `the information is inaccurate, irrelevant, out of date, incomplete or misleading`
                        THEN `the organisation must amend the information`
                        ELSE `HPP 8 does not require an amendment: the information is accurate, relevant, up to date, complete and not misleading`

-- s 34(1) "A private sector person must respond to a request for amendment within 45 days after receiving the
-- request." (5) "taken to have refused to make the amendment requested if [it] fails to respond ... as required".
@export What is the last day for a private health organisation to answer my request to correct my health information (s 34(1))?
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request for amendment
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to answer my amendment request` MEANS `the day it received my request` `plus days` 45

GIVEN `the last day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Health privacy party` `Health privacy act`
`s 34(1) -- a private sector person must respond to a request for amendment within 45 days` `the last day` MEANS
    PARTY `the organisation`
    MUST `respond to the request for amendment`
    WITHIN `the last day`
    LEST BREACH BY `the organisation` BECAUSE "request for amendment not responded to within 45 days after receiving it; taken to have refused (s 34(1), (5))"

@export Has the private health organisation answered my request for correction in time, or is it taken to have refused (s 34(1), (5))?
GIVEN `the day it received my request` IS A DATE @desc The day the organisation received your written request for amendment
      `it has responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has made the amendment or refused it
      `the day it responded` IS A DATE @desc The day it responded (otherwise any date)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `My request's answer`
`Has my amendment request been answered in time` MEANS
    IF `it has responded`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 34(1) -- a private sector person must respond to a request for amendment within 45 days` `the last day`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day answered`), (EVENT `the organisation` `respond to the request for amendment` `the day answered`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the organisation answered within 45 days`
         OTHERWISE `the organisation is taken to have refused my request, because it did not answer within 45 days`
    ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `today` GREATER THAN `the last day`
         THEN `the organisation is taken to have refused my request, because it did not answer within 45 days`
         ELSE `the 45 days have not yet ended`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to answer my amendment request` `the day it received my request`)
        `the day answered` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day it responded`

-- s 35(1) "If a private sector person has refused to amend health information ..., the individual ... may, by notice
-- in writing, require the private sector person to add to the health information a notation". HPP 8(2) "If an
-- organisation is not prepared to amend ..., the organisation must, if so requested by the individual concerned, take
-- such steps as are reasonable to attach to the information ... any statement provided by that individual". A refusal
-- includes one taken to have happened under s 34(5). "If": JUST FALSE before a refusal.
@export The organisation will not correct my health information: can I make it add a note or statement of my claim to the record (HPP 8(2), s 35)?
GIVEN `it has refused or is not prepared to amend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation has refused (including by not answering within 45 days) or is not prepared to make the amendment you asked for
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I make them add my statement to the record` MEANS
    IF `it has refused or is not prepared to amend`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- HPP 8(3) "If health information is amended in accordance with this clause, the individual ... is entitled, if it is
-- reasonably practicable, to have recipients of that information notified of the amendments".
@export My health information was corrected: am I entitled to have the people it was given to told of the correction (HPP 8(3))?
GIVEN `it was amended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation amended your health information under HPP 8
      `notifying the recipients is reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is reasonably practicable to notify those who received the information (answer as you expect a tribunal to)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have the recipients told of the correction` MEANS
    IF `it was amended` AND `notifying the recipients is reasonably practicable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Retention of my health information (s 25)`

-- s 25(1) "A private sector person who is a health service provider must retain health information relating to an
-- individual as follows (a) in the case of health information collected while the individual was an adult -- for 7
-- years from the last occasion on which a health service was provided to the individual by the health service
-- provider, (b) in the case of health information collected while the individual was under the age of 18 years --
-- until the individual has attained the age of 25 years." For public sector agencies and other organisations HPP 5 and
-- the State Records Act 1998 (not deposited) govern: NOTHING. The answer is the day the duty ends: the 7th anniversary
-- of the last service, or the 25th birthday, the day the individual attains 25 (fork F7).
@export On what day does my private health service provider's duty to keep my health information end (s 25(1))?
GIVEN `it is a private sector health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is a private sector health service provider (a private doctor, hospital, dentist, physiotherapist, pharmacy and the like)
      `it was collected while I was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information was collected while you were under 18
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the last day it provided me a health service` IS A DATE @desc The last day the provider gave you a health service
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Until when must my records be kept` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it is a private sector health service provider`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: HPP 5 sets no period and the State Records Act 1998 is not deposited)
    ELSE IF `it was collected while I was under 18`
         THEN JUST (`my date of birth` `plus months` 300)
         ELSE JUST (`the last day it provided me a health service` `plus months` 84)

§§ `Use and disclosure of my health information (HPP 10, HPP 11, s 16)`

-- HPP 10(1) / 11(1) "An organisation that holds health information must not use [disclose] the information for a
-- purpose (a secondary purpose) other than the purpose (the primary purpose) for which it was collected unless" one of
-- the paragraphs holds; HPP 11(1)(g) (compassionate reasons) has no HPP 10 counterpart. HPP 10(2), 11(2): not required
-- to comply if "lawfully authorised or required not to comply" or non-compliance is "otherwise permitted ... under an
-- Act or any other law". s 16(1): nothing in HPP 10 or 11 applies to use or disclosure between members of a group
-- practice "to ensure that a patient ... receives quality health care". Each paragraph's own conditions (consent,
-- reasonable expectation, reasonable belief, de-identification, guidelines) are asked as one fact (fork F8). Use or
-- disclosure for the primary purpose is not limited by these clauses (other HPPs may apply). "unless": JUST FALSE
-- where no paragraph holds.
@export Was the organisation allowed to use or disclose my health information for that purpose (HPP 10, HPP 11, s 16)?
GIVEN `what it did` IS A `Use or disclosure` @desc Whether the organisation used your information itself, or disclosed it to someone else
      `it was for the purpose it was collected for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the use or disclosure was for the same purpose the information was collected for (the primary purpose)
      `the ground relied on` IS A `Ground for use or disclosure` @desc The ground, if any, the organisation relies on for using or disclosing it for another purpose
      `that ground's conditions are met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every condition of that ground holds (for example consent was given; you would reasonably expect it; the organisation reasonably believed it necessary; the information was de-identified or consent was impracticable; any guidelines were followed)
      `it was between members of a group practice for my quality care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was between members of a group practice (shared premises, reception and records by written agreement), to ensure you receive quality health care from them
      `it is lawfully authorised or required not to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation is lawfully authorised or required not to comply with HPP 10 or 11, or an Act or other law otherwise permits it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Were they allowed to use or disclose my health information` MEANS
    IF `it was for the purpose it was collected for`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent: HPP 10 and 11 limit only secondary purposes; other HPPs may still apply)
    ELSE IF `it was between members of a group practice for my quality care` OR `it is lawfully authorised or required not to comply`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE CONSIDER `the ground relied on`
              WHEN `no ground for the use or disclosure is relied on` THEN JUST FALSE
              WHEN `compassionate reasons, to an immediate family member (HPP 11(1)(g))` THEN
                  CONSIDER `what it did`
                  WHEN `disclosure of my information to someone else (HPP 11)` THEN JUST `that ground's conditions are met`
                  OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
              OTHERWISE JUST `that ground's conditions are met`

§§ `Part 6 -- complaining about a private sector organisation`

-- s 42(3) "A complaint must be made within 6 months (or such later time as the Privacy Commissioner may allow) after the
-- time the complainant first became aware of the conduct the subject of the complaint."
@export What is the last day for my complaint to the NSW Privacy Commissioner about a private health organisation (s 42(3))?
GIVEN `the day I first became aware of the conduct` IS A DATE @desc The day you first became aware of the conduct you are complaining about
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to complain` MEANS `the day I first became aware of the conduct` `plus months` 6

GIVEN `the last day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Health privacy party` `Health privacy act`
`s 42(3) -- a complaint must be made within 6 months after first becoming aware` `the last day` MEANS
    PARTY `the individual`
    MUST `make the complaint to the Privacy Commissioner`
    WITHIN `the last day`
    LEST BREACH BY `the individual` BECAUSE "complaint not made within 6 months, or the later time the Privacy Commissioner allowed, after first becoming aware of the conduct (s 42(3))"

@export Is my complaint to the NSW Privacy Commissioner about a private health organisation in time (s 42(3))?
GIVEN `the day I first became aware of the conduct` IS A DATE @desc The day you first became aware of the conduct you are complaining about
      `the Privacy Commissioner allowed a later time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Privacy Commissioner has allowed you a later time
      `the later last day allowed` IS A DATE @desc The last day the Commissioner allowed (otherwise any date)
      `the day I complain` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) your written complaint
GIVETH A `My complaint's timing`
`Is my complaint in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 42(3) -- a complaint must be made within 6 months after first becoming aware` `the last day`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day made`), (EVENT `the individual` `make the complaint to the Privacy Commissioner` `the day made`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `my complaint is in time`
    OTHERWISE `my complaint is late, but the Privacy Commissioner may allow a later time`
    WHERE
        `the six months` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to complain` `the day I first became aware of the conduct`)
        `the last day` MEANS
            IF `the Privacy Commissioner allowed a later time` AND (DATE_SERIAL `the later last day allowed` GREATER THAN `the six months`)
            THEN DATE_SERIAL `the later last day allowed`
            ELSE `the six months`
        `the day made` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day I complain`

-- s 43(2) "The Privacy Commissioner may decide not to deal with a complaint if the Privacy Commissioner is satisfied
-- that (a) ... frivolous, vexatious or lacking in substance, or is not in good faith, or (b) ... trivial, or (c) ...
-- relates to a matter permitted or required by or under any law, or (d) there is available ... an alternative,
-- satisfactory and readily available means of redress, or (e) the matter should be referred [under s 65, 66 or 67],
-- or (f) the person has made a complaint about the same subject matter to the Commonwealth Privacy Commissioner, or to
-- an adjudicator ..., and (i) the complaint has not been withdrawn, or (ii)-(iii) a determination has been made". (3)
-- reasons must be given. "if ... satisfied": JUST FALSE where no ground holds.
@export Can the Privacy Commissioner decline to deal with my complaint (s 43(2))?
GIVEN `the Commissioner is satisfied of a ground in (a) to (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied the complaint is frivolous, vexatious, lacking in substance or not in good faith, trivial, about something the law permits or requires, better redressed elsewhere, or one to refer to the Health Care Complaints Commission or another body
      `my Commonwealth complaint` IS A `My Commonwealth complaint` @desc Whether you have complained about the same matter to the Commonwealth Privacy Commissioner or a privacy code adjudicator, and where that stands
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner decline my complaint` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner is satisfied of a ground in (a) to (e)` OR `a Commonwealth complaint bars it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `a Commonwealth complaint bars it` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my Commonwealth complaint`
            WHEN `I made one and it has not been withdrawn` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `I made one and a determination has been made on it` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 46(2) the Commissioner "may by written notice request the complainant and the respondent to appear ... in
-- conciliation proceedings". (3) "A person or body must not without reasonable excuse fail to comply with the terms of
-- a notice under subsection (2). Maximum penalty -- 50 penalty units in the case of a body corporate or 10 penalty
-- units in any other case." The dollar value of a penalty unit (Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999 s 17) is not
-- deposited: the answer is in penalty units. With a reasonable excuse there is no offence: 0 (fork F9).
@export What is the largest fine, in penalty units, I can face for not attending conciliation the Privacy Commissioner asked me to attend (s 46(3))?
GIVEN `I was given written notice to appear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Privacy Commissioner gave you written notice to appear in conciliation proceedings
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying (a court decides; answer as you expect it to)
      `I am a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a body corporate rather than an individual
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The largest fine for missing conciliation` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I was given written notice to appear`) OR `I had a reasonable excuse`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `I am a body corporate`
         THEN 50
         ELSE 10

-- s 46(4) "The parties to any such conciliation proceedings ... are not entitled to be represented by any other person
-- except by leave of the Privacy Commissioner." s 60(5) the same for inquiries and investigations.
@export Can I have someone, such as a lawyer, represent me before the Privacy Commissioner (ss 46(4), 60(5))?
GIVEN `the Commissioner has given leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Privacy Commissioner has given leave for you to be represented
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be represented before the Commissioner` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner has given leave`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 48(1) "A person who has made a complaint to the Privacy Commissioner under Division 1 may apply to the Tribunal for
-- an inquiry into the complaint, but only if the complaint was the subject of a report of the Privacy Commissioner
-- under section 47." (3) "However, a person cannot apply to the Tribunal if the person has made a complaint about the
-- same subject matter to the Commonwealth Privacy Commissioner, or to an adjudicator ..., and (a) the complaint has not
-- been withdrawn, or (b)-(c) [a determination has been made]".
@export Can I apply to NCAT for an inquiry into my complaint against a private health organisation (s 48(1), (3))?
GIVEN `I complained to the Privacy Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a complaint to the NSW Privacy Commissioner under Part 6
      `the Commissioner made a report on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner investigated further and made a written report on your complaint under section 47 (conciliation, or a decision that it was resolved, is not a report)
      `my Commonwealth complaint` IS A `My Commonwealth complaint` @desc Whether you have complained about the same matter to the Commonwealth Privacy Commissioner or a privacy code adjudicator, and where that stands
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to NCAT` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I complained to the Privacy Commissioner`) OR (NOT `the Commissioner made a report on it`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE CONSIDER `my Commonwealth complaint`
         WHEN `I made one and it has not been withdrawn` THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `I made one and a determination has been made on it` THEN JUST FALSE
         OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 48(2) "An application may only be made within 28 days after (a) the day on which the complainant received the
-- report of the Privacy Commissioner, or (b) the day (if any) recommended in the report ... as the day after which an
-- application may be made to the Tribunal, whichever is later." READING (fork F10): an application before the
-- recommended day is premature (the report recommends the day "after which" an application may be made); and the
-- Tribunal's general power to extend time (Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 s 41) is outside this Act.
@export What is the last day to apply to NCAT after the Privacy Commissioner's report (s 48(2))?
GIVEN `the day I received the report` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the Privacy Commissioner's report
      `the report recommends a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report recommends a day after which an application may be made to NCAT
      `the recommended day` IS A DATE @desc That recommended day (otherwise any date)
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to apply to NCAT` MEANS
    IF `the report recommends a day` AND (`the recommended day` `is after` `the day I received the report`)
    THEN `the recommended day` `plus days` 28
    ELSE `the day I received the report` `plus days` 28

GIVEN `the last day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Health privacy party` `Health privacy act`
`s 48(2) -- an application to the Tribunal may only be made within 28 days` `the last day` MEANS
    PARTY `the individual`
    MUST `apply to the Tribunal`
    WITHIN `the last day`
    LEST BREACH BY `the individual` BECAUSE "application to the Tribunal not made within 28 days after the later of receiving the report and the day it recommends (s 48(2))"

@export Is my application to NCAT about my health privacy complaint in time (s 48(2))?
GIVEN `the day I received the report` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the Privacy Commissioner's report
      `the report recommends a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report recommends a day after which an application may be made to NCAT
      `the recommended day` IS A DATE @desc That recommended day (otherwise any date)
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodge (or lodged) your application with NCAT
GIVETH A `My NCAT application's timing`
`Is my NCAT application in time` MEANS
    IF `the report recommends a day` AND (NOT (`the day I apply` `is after` `the recommended day`))
    THEN `too early: the report recommends a day after which I may apply, and that day has not passed`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 48(2) -- an application to the Tribunal may only be made within 28 days` `the last day`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day applied`), (EVENT `the individual` `apply to the Tribunal` `the day applied`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `my application is in time`
         OTHERWISE `my application is late under section 48(2)`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to apply to NCAT` `the day I received the report` `the report recommends a day` `the recommended day`)
        `the day applied` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the day I apply`

-- s 52(1) the Tribunal "may dismiss the complaint" if satisfied it is "frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking
-- in substance, or that for any other reason the complaint should not be dealt with"; (2) if satisfied "the person does
-- not wish to proceed". (3) "If the Tribunal dismisses a complaint under this section, it may order the complainant to
-- pay the costs of the inquiry." s 53: nothing in s 52 limits the Tribunal's powers under Part 4 of the NCAT Act (whose
-- s 60 costs rule is encoded with that Act).
@export Can NCAT order me to pay the costs of the inquiry into my health privacy complaint (s 52)?
GIVEN `the Tribunal dismissed my complaint under section 52` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether NCAT dismissed your complaint as frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance, for another reason it should not be dealt with, or because you did not wish to proceed
GIVETH A `My costs exposure`
`Can I be ordered to pay the inquiry costs` MEANS
    IF `the Tribunal dismissed my complaint under section 52`
    THEN `the Tribunal may order me to pay the costs of the inquiry (s 52(3))`
    ELSE `section 52(3) does not apply; costs are governed by the Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (s 53)`

-- s 54(1)(a) "subject to subsection (2), an order requiring the respondent to pay to the complainant damages not
-- exceeding $40,000 if the respondent is a body corporate, or not exceeding $10,000 in any other case, by way of
-- compensation for any loss or damage suffered by reason of the respondent's conduct". (2) "only if (a) the application
-- relates to conduct that occurs after the end of the 12-month period following the date on which Schedule 1 commences,
-- and (b) the Tribunal is satisfied that the applicant has suffered financial loss, or psychological or physical harm,
-- because of the conduct". "only if": 0 when either fails. The Tribunal's other orders (s 54(1)(b)-(f)) carry no figure.
@export What is the most NCAT can order the private health organisation to pay me in damages (s 54(1)(a), (2))?
GIVEN `the respondent is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation you complained about is a company or other body corporate (otherwise, for example, an individual practitioner or a partnership)
      `the conduct was after the 12 months following Schedule 1's commencement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct happened after the first 12 months the Health Privacy Principles were in force (after 1 September 2005 for most organisations)
      `the Tribunal is satisfied I suffered loss or harm because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether NCAT is satisfied you suffered financial loss, or psychological or physical harm, because of the organisation's conduct (answer as you expect it to)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most NCAT can award me in damages` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the conduct was after the 12 months following Schedule 1's commencement`) OR (NOT `the Tribunal is satisfied I suffered loss or harm because of it`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `the respondent is a body corporate`
         THEN 40000
         ELSE 10000

§§ `Part 8 -- protection and fees`

-- s 70(1) "A person must not, by threat, intimidation or misrepresentation, persuade or attempt to persuade an
-- individual (a) to refrain from making or pursuing (i) a request for access to health information, or (ii) a
-- complaint to the Privacy Commissioner or the Tribunal under Part 6, or (iii) an application under Part 5 of the PPIP
-- Act ..., or (b) to withdraw such a request, complaint or application. Maximum penalty -- 100 penalty units." (2) "A
-- person must not, by threat, intimidation or false representation, require another person (a) to give a consent under
-- this Act, or (b) to do, without consent, an act for which consent is required. Maximum penalty -- 100 penalty units."
-- The answer is in penalty units; with none of the listed means or aims, s 70 has no offence: 0.
@export What is the largest fine, in penalty units, for someone who threatened or misled me to drop my health information request or complaint, or to consent (s 70)?
GIVEN `what I was pressed to do` IS A `What I was pressed to do` @desc What the person tried to make you do
      `it was by threat, intimidation or misrepresentation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they used a threat, intimidation, a misrepresentation or a false representation
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The largest fine for pressuring me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it was by threat, intimidation or misrepresentation`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE CONSIDER `what I was pressed to do`
         WHEN `something else` THEN 0
         OTHERWISE 100

-- s 73(1) "An organisation may charge a fee for any of the following matters (a) giving an individual a copy of health
-- information, (b) giving an individual an opportunity to inspect and take notes ..., (c) amending health information
-- at the request of an individual, (d) any other matter prescribed by the regulations." (2) "Any fee charged must not
-- exceed such fee (if any) prescribed by the regulations". No regulation is deposited: whether a maximum is prescribed,
-- and what it is, are asked. A matter outside (a)-(d): NOTHING (the list is not closed with "only").
@export Is the organisation allowed to charge me this fee for my health information (s 73)?
GIVEN `what the fee is for` IS A `What the fee is for` @desc What the fee is charged for
      `the regulations prescribe that other matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another matter: whether the regulations prescribe it as a matter a fee may be charged for (otherwise any answer)
      `the fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The fee charged, in dollars
      `the regulations prescribe a maximum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations prescribe a maximum fee for this matter
      `the prescribed maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc That maximum, in dollars (otherwise any number)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can they charge me this fee` MEANS
    IF `the matter is listed`
    THEN IF `the regulations prescribe a maximum` AND (`the fee` GREATER THAN `the prescribed maximum`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the list in s 73(1) is not closed with "only": the Act is silent on other fees)
    WHERE
        `the matter is listed` MEANS
            CONSIDER `what the fee is for`
            WHEN `another matter` THEN `the regulations prescribe that other matter`
            OTHERWISE TRUE
